Empty workspace. I'll scaffold a minimal vite + react + vitest + testing-library project in /workspace/countdown.

Plan:
- package.json with deps: react, react-dom, @types/react; dev: vitest, @testing-library/react, @testing-library/dom, @testing-library/jest-dom, jsdom, @vitejs/plugin-react, typescript.
- Countdown.tsx: takes seconds, onExpire. Renders <time> with mm:ss. setInterval 1000ms. Pauses/resumes via buttons. Avoid stale closure: use functional setState and read latest paused via ref (or better: interval callback checks pausedRef). Classic bug: `useEffect(() => { const id = setInterval(() => { if (!paused) setSeconds(s => s - 1) }, 1000); return clear }, [])` — the paused here is stale. Guard by using a ref synced with state.

Also ensure onExpire fires exactly once: use a ref flag or fire when seconds reaches 0 inside updater? Firing side effects inside updater is bad. Better: derive remaining from deadline timestamps? Simpler robust approach: keep remaining in state, tick decrements only if not expired. Fire onExpire in an effect when remaining hits 0, guarded by a ref so it fires once even under React strict mode double-invocation... Effects run twice in StrictMode dev; tests won't use StrictMode but guard anyway with firedRef.

Implementation:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

function format(totalSeconds: number): string {
  const m = Math.floor(totalSeconds / 60);
  const s = totalSeconds % 60;
  return `${String(m).padStart(2, "0")}:${String(s).padStart(2, "0")}`;
}

export function Countdown({ seconds, onExpire }: { seconds: number; onExpire?: () => void }) {
  const [remaining, setRemaining] = useState(seconds);
  const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);
  const pausedRef = useRef(paused);
  const expiredRef = useRef(false);

  useEffect(() => { pausedRef.current = paused; }, [paused]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      if (!pausedRef.current && !expiredRef.current) {
        setRemaining(prev => Math.max(0, prev - 1));
      }
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (remaining === 0 && !expiredRef.current) {
      expiredRef.current = true;
      onExpire?.();
    }
  }, [remaining, onExpire]);

  return (
    <div>
      <time>{format(remaining)}</time>
      <button onClick={() => setPaused(true)} disabled={paused || remaining === 0}>Pause</button>
      <button onClick={() => setPaused(false)} disabled={!paused}>Resume</button>
      {remaining === 0 ? "Expired" : null}
    </div>
  );
}
```

The stale-closure guard: interval created once, reads pausedRef.current always fresh. Test covering pause immediately after tick boundary: advance to just after a tick (e.g., advanceTimersByTime to right after a second boundary), click Pause synchronously, then advance time across multiple boundaries, assert display unchanged.

